A profile name is checked in the browser and then accepted by the service without any check at all.

Code sample (written for this course):

// the browser, on the customer's machine
btn.onclick = () => { if (name.value.length > 0) submit(name.value); };

// the service, which receives the same field
app.post("/profile", (req, res) => {
  save(req.body.name);
  res.json({ ok: true });
});

The check above runs in the browser and the same field is accepted on the service with no check at all. Where is the boundary, and where does the check belong?

An ordering report takes a caller's choice of column and splices it into the statement, and a second version of the same report tries a marker instead.

Code sample (written for this course):

app.get("/orders", (req, res) => {
  const sort = req.query.sort;              // caller chooses: "created" or "total"
  const sql = "SELECT id, total FROM orders ORDER BY " + sort + " ASC LIMIT 50";
  res.json(db.prepare(sql).all());
});

// the same report, written with a marker at the sort position
const sql2 = "SELECT id, total FROM orders ORDER BY ? ASC LIMIT 50";
db.prepare(sql2).all(sort);

The code above runs both forms against the same table. What does the second form actually do, and what follows for how the choice must be made?

One endpoint uses the data mapper correctly and a second endpoint on the same service builds its statement by hand.

Code sample (written for this course):

// the ordinary path: the mapper builds the statement and binds every value
const rows = mapper.query("Order", { where: { customerId }, sort: "created DESC" });

// the reporting endpoint, added later because the mapper has no field for one column
const rows2 = mapper.raw(
  "SELECT id, total FROM orders WHERE customerId = '" + customerId + "' ORDER BY " + sort
);

The mapper is careful with every value it is given. What does the second line reopen, and why does the first line not have the same problem?

A project-wide encoder for element text is used to write a stored name into a quoted attribute on a page.

Code sample (written for this course):

// the encoder this project uses everywhere
const text = (s) => s.replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;");

// a stored display name, written into a quoted attribute
res.send('<span title="' + text(row.name) + '">profile</span>');

// the entry check, for reference
// if (!/^[A-Za-z ]{1,40}$/.test(name)) throw new BadRequest();

A name is checked on the way in against letters and spaces only. What happens to a name of x" onmouseover="steal() that was stored before that check existed?

A middleware line copies whatever origin arrived into the response header on every request the service answers.

Code sample (written for this course):

// applied to every response this service sends
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", req.headers.origin);
  res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Credentials", "true");
  res.setHeader("Access-Control-Allow-Headers", "authorization, content-type");
  next();
});

Run against a local test service, this answers with the caller's own origin whatever that origin is. What does it permit, and where was the decision made?

An endpoint accepts a state blob from a caller and rebuilds live objects from a description inside it.

Code sample (written for this course):

// the endpoint, which authenticates nobody before it accepts the blob
app.post("/cart", (req, res) => {
  res.json(restore(req.body.blob));
});

// restore turns a description back into live objects
const TYPES = { Coupon: Coupon, Voucher: Voucher, GiftCard: GiftCard };
function restore(bytes) {
  const { type, args } = decode(bytes);
  return new TYPES[type](...args);
}

The routine above rebuilds objects from a description the caller supplied. What is the flaw, and what is the repair that removes it rather than narrowing it?

Two versions of a reporting call pass the same file name to another program, one as text and one as a list.

Code sample (written for this course):

const file = req.query.name;                 // the caller supplies: "report one.txt"

// form one: a single string, handed to a shell
exec("wkhtmltopdf /reports/" + file + " /out/" + file);

// form two: the program and its arguments, passed separately
execFile("wkhtmltopdf", ["/reports/" + file, "/out/" + file]);

// the program prints what it received as arguments
// process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(process.argv.slice(1)))

Run against a throwaway directory where a report exists whose name contains a space, what does each form pass to the program?

A document directory holds one real file and one symbolic link pointing outside it, and a handler checks names before serving.

Code sample (written for this course):

// the document directory, as it exists on the server
//   /srv/public/handbook.txt      a real file
//   /srv/public/link.txt          a link pointing at /srv/private/keys.txt

const root = "/srv/public";
const safe = (p) => {
  const full = path.resolve(root, p);
  return full.startsWith(path.resolve(root) + path.sep);
};

app.get("/doc/:name", (req, res) => {
  if (!safe(req.params.name)) return res.status(400).end();
  res.sendFile(path.join(root, req.params.name));
});

A request for link.txt passes the check above. Does the check hold, and what would it take for it to hold?
